A||||7||Archie||440T4 Axles?||||5||
Z||000000||Archie||12-27-2000||10:30 AM||||One of my non-Internet friends (yes there are still people who are not on the Internet) is working on installing a 440T4 into his 1987 V-6 powered Fiero. He is leaving the engine in the original position.<P>He was asking me what axles he needs to use. I know this subject comes up all the time but I seldom pay attention to it. I've also done some searches on the subject but I don't find the answer.<P>The question is..... What is this guy going to have to do for axles?<P>Archie||||reg||5
Z||000001||william_thorniii||12-27-2000||11:05 AM||||Archie,<P>Many folks go throung Moser Engineering in IN for custom axles.  Either Summit or Jegs also claim that they can get you any length with any spline necessary.  I was going to try them first.  <P>Keep us posted.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||3
Z||000002||Old Lar||12-27-2000||11:53 AM||||Archie:  Bob Steiger (Twin Lakes Fiero) is performing that swap and he may give up his axle method.  All he said to me was he needed to make the axles for the swap.  Since I don't have the skill, tools, space or materials, I let him do the work because he knows what he's doing.||||reg||3
Z||000003||terryk||12-27-2000||01:12 PM||||Seems to me, a set of stock A-car axle assemblies (from a 440/4T60/Non-E application) with manual trans outer CV's swapped on would work considering A-car 4T60E axles worked on my 86 SE for the Buick SC swap. <P>Terry||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||12-27-2000||02:28 PM||||There are a bunch of threads about this. I forget exactly which axles are involved but they are listed in the forum here in at least a couple threads. It's like Terry said... You take inner CV and axles from some other GM appication(s) and put on Fiero outer CV's from a manual tanny setup. I just can't remember what axles where used there were a couple differant choices.<P>The parts were common. It's just a matter of hooking them up. no custom cutting anything.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 12-27-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||p4n1c||12-27-2000||02:48 PM||||The '88-'92 W-bodies(Grand Prix, Lumina, Cutlass Supreme, Regal) use TH440 auto transmissions.  Maybe you could start looking there?  The '88-'89s used 2.8s.<P>------------------<BR>p4n1c<BR>Come out swingin'||||reg||1
Z||000006||terryk||12-27-2000||08:48 PM||||Let me be more specific..<P>Use an axle pair from a 1986-92 A car (Ciera/Century/6000)with a 3800/3.8L and 4spd automatic (440/4T60). Remove the outer CV's and replace them with the outer CV's from a manual transmission Fiero. The automatic Fiero CV's won't work because they are smaller axles.<P>This assumes the 440 has been positioned the same as the 125C in the cradle. The cradle will need new slots added on the front for the front trans mount. Add them in the same location as the rear. Notice the rear has two sets of slots? The front doesn't. Use 86 Olds Ciera 440 trans brackets and rubber mounts. That should place it exactly where the 125 or manual trans was.<P>You should be able to call any of the CV places and get a set already done. Just tell them you want 86 Olds Ciera axles with a 4spd auto but with 86 Fiero manual transmission outer CV's. That gives them specific reference numbers.<P>CV's Unlimited did mine for $80 a side. This was for the 4T60E which uses a slightly shorter passenger side axle. You need the 440/4T60 shaft.<P>Terry ||||reg||1
Z||000007||bubbajoexx||12-28-2000||04:01 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Archie:<BR><B>One of my non-Internet friends (yes there are still people who are not on the Internet) is working on installing a 440T4 into his 1987 V-6 powered Fiero. He is leaving the engine in the original position.<P>He was asking me what axles he needs to use. I know this subject comes up all the time but I seldom pay attention to it. I've also done some searches on the subject but I don't find the answer.<P>The question is..... What is this guy going to have to do for axles?<P>Archie</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Use the stock auto axles they fit right in under slight compesion about 1/4 of an inch on each side and use the 89 pontiac 6000 mounts they fit perfect in the setup.Use of after market axles is un nessary<BR><p>[This message has been edited by bubbajoexx (edited 12-28-2000).]||||reg||1
